assertion  XSH.frexp.001
anchor     functions/frexp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/frexp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       frexp(8), (1), (-6), (0.1875), (1024), (0.5), (3) and (-0.25) give the
           fractions 0.5, 0.5, -0.75, 0.75, 0.5, 0.5, 0.75 and -0.5 with
           exponents 4, 1, 3, -2, 11, 0, 2 and -1.
note       Q-109: the values are those of a binary radix.
test       t/001-values.c

assertion  XSH.frexp.002
anchor     functions/frexp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/frexp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       For the largest finite value and the smallest normal value of the
           type, the fraction has magnitude in [1/2, 1) and fraction times two to
           the exponent restores the argument.
note       The value is rebuilt by repeated doubling or halving, which is exact
           for these magnitudes. Q-109: binary radix.
test       t/002-extremes.c

assertion  XSH.frexp.003
anchor     functions/frexp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/frexp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       frexp(+0) and frexp(-0) return a zero and, where the IEC 60559
           Floating-Point option is claimed, store 0 in the exponent object
           (which held 7 before).
note       The tag on the exponent sentence is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point
           option; the zero result itself is required everywhere (the general
           rule allows a zero fraction), the exponent check applies only where
           the C implementation claims the option (Annex F of the C standard).
test       t/003-zero.c

assertion  XSH.frexp.004
anchor     functions/frexp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/frexp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       frexp(+0) returns +0 and frexp(-0) returns -0.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/004-zero-sign.c

assertion  XSH.frexp.005
anchor     functions/frexp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/frexp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       frexp(NaN, &e) returns a NaN (the exponent object is unspecified).
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/005-nan.c

assertion  XSH.frexp.006
anchor     functions/frexp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/frexp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       frexp(+Inf, &e) returns +Inf and frexp(-Inf, &e) returns -Inf (the
           exponent object is unspecified).
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/006-infinity.c

assertion  XSH.frexp.007
anchor     functions/frexp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/frexp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       With each rounding direction that can be selected (at least two are
           needed), frexp(10) returns 0.625 with exponent 4 and frexp(-10)
           returns -0.625 with exponent 4 (a binary radix).
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard). The page requires exactness when the radix is a power of
           two; the test needs FLT_RADIX 2.
test       t/007-rounding-mode.c

assertion  XSH.frexp.008
anchor     functions/frexp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/frexp.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.23
class      required
since      1988
says       With errno holding EDOM before the call, frexp(8, &e) leaves it
           unchanged and, when exception flags are in use, raises none of the
           invalid, divide-by-zero and overflow exceptions.
test       t/008-no-error.c
